{"title": "CSCI-251: Concepts of Parallel and Distributed Systems", "learningObjectives": [
     "Define the core architectural models of parallel and distributed systems, including multi-core, clusters, grids, client-server, and peer-to-peer topol",
     "Explain the fundamental communication and synchronization mechanisms required for correct concurrent execution in shared and distributed memory",
     "Apply practical analysis methods to evaluate performance bottlenecks, such as communication overhead, load imbalance, and synchronization costs, in a "
   ], "beats": [
     {
       "title": "Foundations of Parallel Architectures",
       "claim": "Parallel systems are organized into distinct models including multi-core processors, parallel clusters, computing grids, client-server architectures, and peer-to-peer networks. [ri",
       "example": "A multi-core processor shares a single memory space, while a distributed cluster connects independent machines over a network to solve large-scale problems. [ri-csci-251]",
       "visualDirection": "Display a side-by-side diagram comparing a single multi-core CPU with a networked cluster of independent nodes, highlighting memory locality versus network communication. [ri-csci-251]",
       "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-251"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Distributed-Memory Process Organization",
       "claim": "Distributed-memory programs must partition data and work across processes, communicate required dependencies, and avoid deadlock to ensure correctness. [mpi-standard]",
       "example": "In a MPI program, each process holds a unique segment of an array and must explicitly send and receive data to complete a global computation. [mpi-standard]",
       "visualDirection": "Show a flowchart of data partitioning across four processes, with arrows indicating message passing and a highlighted deadlock scenario with circular waits. [mpi-standard]",
       "sourceIds": ["mpi-standard"]
     },
     {
       "title": "MPI Communication Mechanisms",
       "claim": "MPI defines communication among processes identified by ranks in communicators, using point-to-point operations for typed messages and collective operations for coordinated group. ",
       "example": "A broadcast sends a single message from one rank to all others, while a reduction combines values from all ranks into a single result. [mpi-standard]",
       "visualDirection": "Render a star topology for a broadcast and a tree topology for a reduction, labeling each node with its rank and the direction of data flow. [mpi-standard]",
       "sourceIds": ["mpi-standard"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Blocking and Nonblocking Operations",
       "claim": "Blocking and nonblocking operations expose different completion and overlap behavior, allowing programmers to control when communication completes relative to computation. [mpi",
       "example": "A nonblocking send allows a process to continue executing independent tasks while the message is transmitted, improving pipeline efficiency. [mpi-standard]",
       "visualDirection": "Illustrate a timeline showing a blocking operation halting execution until completion versus a nonblocking operation allowing overlapping computation and communication. [mpi-standard]",
       "sourceIds": ["mpi-standard"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Shared-Memory Parallel Programming",
       "claim": "OpenMP defines a shared-memory parallel programming model based on directives, library routines, and environment variables that create a team of threads. [openmp-specification]",
       "example": "A parallel region directive spawns multiple threads that execute concurrently within the same address space, sharing variables unless explicitly marked otherwise. [openmp-specification]",
       "visualDirection": "Draw a single CPU core with multiple thread execution contexts branching from a parallel region, showing shared vs. private variable scopes. [openmp-specification]",
       "sourceIds": ["openmp-specification"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Worksharing and Data Sharing",
       "claim": "Worksharing constructs divide loop iterations or sections among the team, while data-sharing attributes determine whether variables are shared, private, firstprivate, or reduced. [",
       "example": "A firstprivate clause copies a variable to each thread, ensuring independent execution without race conditions, whereas a shared clause allows concurrent access. [openmp-specification]",
       "visualDirection": "Present a table mapping data-sharing attributes to thread behavior, with icons indicating private copies versus shared memory access. [openmp-specification]",
       "sourceIds": ["openmp-specification"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Synchronization and Data Races",
       "claim": "Barriers and other synchronization constructs establish ordering where independent execution is not sufficient, and incorrectly synchronized accesses can create data races. [openmp",
       "example": "A barrier forces all threads to wait until every thread reaches the same point, preventing one thread from reading uninitialized data. [openmp-specification]",
       "visualDirection": "Show a race condition scenario where two threads write to the same variable without a barrier, resulting in corrupted output, then show the corrected version with a barrier. [openmp-specification]",
       "sourceIds": ["openmp-specification"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Performance Analysis Metrics",
       "claim": "Performance analysis considers communication volume, latency, load balance, synchronization, process placement, and the ratio of useful work to coordination overhead. [mpi-standard",
       "example": "A poorly load-balanced cluster wastes resources when some nodes finish early and idle while others process remaining work. [mpi-standard]",
       "visualDirection": "Create a bar chart comparing communication overhead versus useful computation across different process placements, highlighting the optimal ratio. [mpi-standard]",
       "sourceIds": ["mpi-standard"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Scalability and Concurrency Evaluation",
       "claim": "Parallel analysis distinguishes available concurrency, task granularity, dependencies, synchronization cost, memory behavior, and measured scalability to guide system design. [open",
       "example": "Fine-grained tasks increase concurrency but raise synchronization costs, while coarse-grained tasks reduce overhead but limit available concurrency. [openmp-specification]",
       "visualDirection": "Plot a graph showing scalability efficiency against task granularity, marking the optimal region where synchronization cost is minimized. [openmp-specification]",
       "sourceIds": ["openmp-specification"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist",
       "claim": "Students must verify that data partitioning avoids deadlock, communication patterns match the chosen model, and synchronization prevents data races before deployment. [ri-csci-251]",
       "example": "A checklist confirms that MPI ranks are correctly mapped, OpenMP barriers are placed after shared variable updates, and load distribution is balanced across all nodes. [ri-csci-251]",
       "visualDirection": "Display a printable checklist template with checkboxes for deadlock avoidance, model alignment, and race condition prevention, ready for student use. [ri-csci-251]",
       "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-251"]
     }
   ]}
